# What a minified build with live calls (`-PliveCalls=true` on a release-shaped build; docs/VOICE_CALLS.md) adds to R8's
# rules. The build adds this directory to a variant only where `BuildConfig.LIVE_CALLS` is on, so a shipped artifact
# never reads it. The webrtc-sdk AAR ships no consumer rules, yet libwebrtc's native half finds its Java half by name: it
# calls `@CalledByNative` methods, constructs `org.webrtc` classes and dispatches to observers we implement (the peer
# connection's, `DirectNetworkDetector`'s). Renaming or dropping any of them fails inside native code, not in a test,
# so the whole package is pinned, as upstream's own sample app does.
-keep class org.webrtc.** { *; }
# The AAR's other package, Chromium's JNI runtime. `JNI_OnLoad` looks `org.jni_zero.JniZero` up by name and calls its
# private `init()`, which no Java code reaches, so R8 drops the package and the first `PeerConnectionFactory.initialize`
# aborts the process (`java_class == null in call to GetStaticMethodID`, GitHub #40: every call on 2.10.0-alpha.0).
# Only what native calls is pinned, by jni_zero's own marker, as its upstream rules do: the whole package would keep
# `setJniClassLoader`, whose generated `JniZeroJni` the AAR does not ship, and R8 refuses the missing class.
-keepclasseswithmembers class org.jni_zero.** {
    @org.jni_zero.CalledByNative <methods>;
}
